# DI Registration

All DI wiring lives in `LoadlyAggregator.Infrastructure/Extensions/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs`. Register every new type you add; the codebase does not use assembly scanning for domain components.

## What to register and how

| Component                                       | Lifetime  | Interface vs concrete                         |
|-------------------------------------------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------|
| Repositories                                    | Scoped    | `AddScoped<IXRepository, XRepository>()`      |
| Domain Actions                                  | Scoped    | `AddScoped<CreateXAction>()` (concrete)       |
| Filter utilities (`XFilterUtility`)             | Scoped    | `AddScoped<XFilterUtility>()` (concrete)      |
| Domain services with an interface               | Scoped    | `AddScoped<IXService, XService>()`            |
| Stateless strategy / builder / resolver helpers | Scoped    | concrete; or Transient if truly per-call      |
| Options / configuration binders                 | Singleton | `services.Configure<XOptions>(...)`           |
| `DbContext`, `IUnitOfWork`                      | Scoped    | already wired — don't duplicate               |

Actions are registered by **concrete type** because controllers inject them by concrete type (no interface).

## Example

```csharp
// Repository
services.AddScoped<IInvoiceRepository, InvoiceRepository>();

// Actions
services.AddScoped<CreateInvoiceAction>();
services.AddScoped<ListInvoicesAction>();

// Filter utility
services.AddScoped<InvoicesFilterUtility>();
```

## Checklist when adding a new feature

- [ ] Repository + its interface registered.
- [ ] Each Action class registered.
- [ ] Filter utility (if a List action was added) registered.
- [ ] Any new validator does **not** need manual registration — FluentValidation is auto-discovered.
- [ ] Lifetime matches dependencies (don't put a Singleton on top of a Scoped `DbContext`).
